sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 3, docs/reference/task-containers.md): on a container host, the refresh keeps one stopped base container, `HOST-base`, that never gets an identity. Its ZFS snapshot is the base a task start copies. The workflow and step names stay those of docs/cli/task/vm-image-refresh.mmd; the intent carries `runtime: container`, and the Ansible phases branch on it. The base holds the Pi server, the tools, a warm checkout and the nested fleet in an inner Incus with a directory pool: the ready copies. There is no seed volume.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · in every Gateway release, and daily
  %% kind: task_vm.image_refresh
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane BC icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve · Swamp Node
  participant H as Container host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant BC as Base container<br/>HOST-base
  Note over GW: Admit the refresh<br/>one per host and runtime
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - every Gateway release, after the Swamp load, and the daily schedule; the same triggers as a VM host
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a host with `settings.task_vms.containers` gets a container refresh beside its VM refresh: its own run, intent `runtime: container`, its own base and its own queue; a host without a recorded container base builds it from stock
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-image-refresh
  SW->>H: Prepare the host<br/>shared Ansible phase
  %%> What it does, for a container host:
  %%> - the pool, the bridge `orbittaskc0` with its egress ACL and port isolation, the forwarding rule and the DHCP and DNS input rules, as for a VM host
  %%> - the project `orbit-task-containers` with `restricted.containers.nesting=allow`, `restricted.containers.interception=allow`, `restricted.containers.privilege=isolated`, `restricted.containers.lowlevel=allow` (for `security.idmap.size`) and `restricted.snapshots=allow`; host-path disks stay forbidden, so no container can mount the host's Incus socket
  %%> - the stock image alias `ubuntu-26.04-ct` from `images:ubuntu/26.04/cloud`, the container variant
  %%> - the kernel modules the containers need, loaded now and at boot, and the host sysctls; root's subordinate ID range must hold one isolated range per place and one for the base, or the phase fails
  %%> - the pool must be ZFS; `zfs_arc_max` is capped to the host; Incus comes from the channel the recipe pins; `sctp` is blocked
  %%> - a small isolated test copy must write under a few MB (`zfs get written`), which proves idmapped mounts; Incus 7 reports no `kernel_features`
  %%> - every `incus` call closes standard input, which `incus` reads as YAML when it is not a terminal
  SW->>H: Log egress<br/>host bridge, log only
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the egress log of the container bridge, the same part as for a VM host
  SW->>H: Start the base VM<br/>incus start
  %%> What it does, for a container host:
  %%> - starts the stopped base container; its nested containers start with it
  %%> - a host without a recorded container base runs `incus init` from `ubuntu-26.04-ct` with nesting, intercepted `mknod` and `setxattr`, an isolated ID map of 101,000,000 IDs, `boot.autostart=false` and the profile of the project, then starts it
  %%> - the step keeps its VM name, because the workflow is shared
  SW->>BC: Converge<br/>the same converge as a task start
  %%> What it does, for a container:
  %%> - software and tools as on a VM, with the Pi server; the tools phase also installs the inner Incus, writes `/etc/subuid` and `/etc/subgid` as `root:1000000:100000000`, and makes the inner directory pool `topology-seed` and the network `oe-nested`
  %%> - the boot phase sets up WireGuard with `ip` and `wg` in `orbit-wireguard.service`, not `wg-quick`, and gives the identity's units `ConditionPathExists=` on their files; no QEMU config and no identity unit
  %%> - the fleet phase builds a missing fleet once: three nested containers with nesting and a read-only device that shows `N` at `/sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled`, then the nested Gateway, app-dev, app-prod and the operator join, as on a VM; the operator tunnel `wg-nested` runs as `orbit-wg-nested.service`, with `ip` and `wg`
  %%> - the task "Take the base snapshots" stops the nested containers before it snapshots them, then starts them; on a directory pool a snapshot of a running container is not consistent
  %%> - the fleet check fails on any unit in the fleet that uses `LoadCredential`
  SW->>H: Stop and snapshot<br/>nested first, then base
  %%> What it does, for a container:
  %%> - stops the three nested containers inside the base, then stops the base cleanly, then snapshots it under the run's name; one ZFS snapshot carries the whole fleet, so no database page is half written
  SW->>H: Prove the candidate<br/>one task start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one task start from the new snapshot in a free CI place of this host, then its release
  SW->>H: Prune old snapshots<br/>keep -previous
  SW->>H: Remove host leftovers<br/>after the prune
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same cleanup, in the container project
  alt the run passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>new snapshot
    Note over GW: Promote<br/>record the new snapshot
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task starts on this host copy the new snapshot; the build row records the runtime
  else a step failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the base container stops and rolls back to the recorded snapshot; task starts keep it
  end
